          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.495,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.136,
            0.878,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "|  | Day 1 | Day 2 | Day 3 | Day 4 | Day 5 | Weekly Average |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Week 1 | 10 | 13 | 10 | 15 | 16 | 12.8 |\n| Week 2 | 14 | 10 | 20 | 17 | 18 | 15.8 |\n| Week 3 | 15 | 18 | 14 | 20 | 23 | 18 |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.201,
                0.11,
                0.797,
                0.131
              ],
              "content": "Table 10.1: Time spent cycling, in minutes, by Jaspreet"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_table_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.243,
            0.591,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "Two ways of calculating this are given here."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.267,
            0.876,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c} \\begin{array}{c}\\text { Method 1 } \\\\\\text {set } \\\\\\underbrace{(12.8+15.8+18)}_{3} \\\\=\\underbrace{46.63}_{3} \\\\=15.53\\end{array} \\quad \\text {set } \\\\\\underbrace{(12.8 \\times 5)+(15.8 \\times 5)+(18 \\times 5)}_{5+5+5} \\\\=\\underbrace{(64+79+90)}_{15} \\\\以 \\\\\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.391,
            0.881,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "The average time spent cycling by Jaspreet on weekdays is 15.53 minutes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.41,
            0.881,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "We saw earlier how Method 1 (badminton example) did not produce the correct value. Why does Method 1 give the correct answer in this case? When does Method 1 work and when does it not work? Let us find out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.466,
            0.813,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "In general, the average of data in three collections is given as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.491,
            0.881,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\left(Sum of values in collection 1\\right) \\div \\left(Sum of values in collection 2\\right) \\div \\left(Sum of values in collection 3\\right)}_{\\left(No. of values in collection 1\\right) \\div \\left(No. of values in collection 2\\right) \\div \\left(No. of values in collection 3\\right)} \\\\\\underbrace{=ap + by + cr}_{\\begin{aligned}p + q + r \\end{aligned}},\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.575,
            0.881,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "where a, b, c are the averages of each collection, and p, q, r are the sizes of the respective collections."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.612,
            0.882,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "In the cycling scenario, Jaspreet cycled 5 days every week for 3 weeks. This means that all three collections are of the same size, that is 5. Thus, in the general form, the average becomes,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.674,
            0.788,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ap + bp + cp}{p + p + p} & ( where   p   is the size of the collections)  \\\\= \\frac{(a + b + c)p}{3p} = & \\frac{a + b + c}{3}.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.761,
            0.883,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "This is the same as adding the 3 weekly averages and dividing the sum by 3. Here, each week has 5 days, so each average comes from the same number of days; that is why treating each week’s average equally works. If the weeks had different numbers of cycling days, this method would no longer be correct."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.854,
            0.883,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "We see that when the sizes of the collections are the same, to find the average of the combined collection, we can add the averages of the collections and divide the sum by the number of collections."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
